   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>It's generally impossible to check if a pointer is \"valid\", given only the pointer. Certainly, calling delete on an \"invalid\" pointer will not throw an exception, except perhaps via undefined behaviour. </p>\n\n<p>Basically, your code needs to explicitly track whether a pointer is \"valid\" or not. Or better use smart pointers, such as std::unique_ptr.</p>\n"
  },
