   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/44424820/#comment75850021_44424843",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2017-06-08T02:56:36Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "catch(...) is suppose to catch everything that is thrown. how to catch/detect undefined behaviour before program crashes?"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 75851339,
   "parent_id": 44424843,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 4581301,
   "author_display_name": "user4581301",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/44424820/#comment75851339_44424843",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2017-06-08T04:32:58Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "@michaeleric yes, but that requires something to have been thrown. <code>free</code> is a good ol&#39; C function and C knows not what an exception is and thus cannot throw an exception. You also cannot catch signals such as segfaults and floating point exceptions (because it turns out FPE isn&#39;t an exception in the C++ sense), asserts, aborts, and many other forms of program-crashing ninjitsu. Most of the time you don&#39;t want to intercept, handle them, and continue because they mean your program is utterly hosed and should be allowed to die peacefully."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 75854100,
   "parent_id": 44424820,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 1450890,
   "author_display_name": "Rook",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/44424820/#comment75854100_44424820",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2017-06-08T06:23:08Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "You&#39;ll probably find that &quot;<i>free(): invalid pointer</i>&quot; is not actually an exception but another error reporting mechanism that cannot be caught, like a debug assertion. It is probably generated by non-c++ code, for a start."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 75861373,
   "parent_id": 44424843,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 207421,
   "author_display_name": "user207421",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/44424820/#comment75861373_44424843",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2017-06-08T09:34:56Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "@michaeleric The point is that there is no guarantee that undefined behaviour will throw an exception in the first place, so you can write whatever code you like to cope with undefined behaviour but there is nothing defined that says your code will ever be invoked. The solution is not to write more code, it is to fix the code that engages in undefined behaviour in the first place."
  }
